timeclock-system: bozorth3 safe from any number of threads at once, and cancellable.

bozorth3 keeps its working tables in globals, and every score is two steps over them (bozorth_probe_init fills them
from the probe, bozorth_to_gallery reads them per comparison), so two threads matching at once overwrite each other's
tables and return wrong scores silently. This makes every mutable table thread-local: each thread gets its own copy
(about 47 MB once touched), allocated by the C runtime when the thread first matches. It also lets
fp_print_score_press's caller stop a call from another thread: a GCancellable, checked before each of the other print's
presses and in every loop whose length the minutiae decide (bz_comp, bz_match, bz_match_score's outer and inner loops,
bz_final_loop), which return early with nothing to clean up, as their own overflow returns already do. A thread can't
be stopped safely from outside (a kill or a signal handler jumping out can leave the allocator's or GLib's locks held
for good), so the call stops itself.

A thread's tables are freed with its thread-local block, which glibc frees only when it drops the thread's stack:
glibc caches exited threads' stacks (40 MB of them by default) for reuse, blocks included, so a process whose matcher
threads come and go must start with GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.pthread.stack_cache_size=0, or a few exited threads' tables
stay resident. glibc reads it once, at process start, so this library can't set it; timeclock-system's image sets it
and its app restarts itself with it when missing. Tables on the heap, freed at thread exit, would need no setting but
matched about 4x slower.

Audit of every global and static the matcher can reach:
- Thread-local (written while matching):
  - bz_gbls.c, both TARGET_OS branches, with their externs in nbis/include/bozorth.h: colp, scols, fcols, scolpt,
    fcolpt, sc, yl, rq, tq, zz, rx, mm, nn, qq, rk, cp, rp, rf, cf, bz_y.
  - bozorth3.c, file scope: ct, gct, ctt, ctp, yy.
  - bozorth3.c, inside functions: rot and rtp (bz_match), sct (bz_final_loop).
- Read-only: ii_table (bozorth3.c, bz_match): an initialised constant table, never written.
- Never written in libfprint's build:
  - verbose_threshold: declared extern in bozorth.h, defined and used nowhere.
  - bz_io.c's program_buffer, pfile, gfile: the command-line tool's state, only read back by get_progname,
    get_probe_filename and get_gallery_filename; nothing in libfprint sets them.
- bz_alloc.c, bz_drvrs.c, bz_sort.c: no globals or statics.
- libfprint's glue (fpi-print.c's fpi_print_bz3_match), identify-best-match.patch and registration-presses.patch: locals
  only, plus FpPrint fields (identify_score) owned by the caller's object; each thread uses its own FpPrint objects.
- mindtct, which extracts minutiae during a capture, isn't touched: only the device's thread runs it.

